(window.FAIAS_IRQ=window.FAIAS_IRQ||{})["IRQ-MOD-016"]={"canvas":[{"n":1,"title":"IRQ ID & Title","html":"<p><strong>IRQ-MOD-016: Pre-Deployment Evaluation Evidence Without Established External Validity Against Deployment Conditions</strong></p>\n<p>Register question: \"Where the institution relies on its own pre-deployment testing, UAT, pilot or vendor evaluation results to support an authorisation decision, has it established that those results are a valid proxy for behaviour under deployment conditions — documenting the respects in which the evaluation environment diverges from the production context, reporting the rate at which the system's own traces indicate it is reasoning about being tested, graded or monitored, reporting the consequential results split by that indication, and stating in the authorisation record what the pre-deployment evidence can and cannot carry?\"</p>\n<p><strong>Applicability:</strong> Y / Y / N. Predictive-N with a stated exception: the mechanism requires the system to condition behaviour on inferred context, which a predictive model does not do; the marking flips to Y where a predictive stack samples at inference or is wrapped in a generative layer (the applicability-conditional convention recorded for IRQ-MOD-014 in REGISTER_STATS.md).</p>"},{"n":2,"title":"Business Activities Affected","html":"<ul>\n<li><strong>Model and system authorisation:</strong> a lending, AML, claims, suitability or onboarding system is approved on the strength of UAT, internal evaluation or pilot results; the approval paper treats those results as evidence about deployment behaviour without stating why they are a valid proxy for it.</li>\n<li><strong>Vendor and model selection:</strong> candidate models or prompt configurations are compared on the firm's own evaluation harness, and the comparison is carried into procurement as though it predicted relative behaviour in the live process.</li>\n<li><strong>Post-approval reporting:</strong> committee and board papers cite the pre-deployment result as the standing evidence of capability or safety, with no reconciliation against the realised metric once the system is live.</li>\n<li><strong>Provider-evidence reliance:</strong> the firm relies on a provider's published safety evaluations for the model version it has deployed, without reconciling those results against its own evaluation conditions (links IRQ-TPR-001).</li>\n</ul>"},{"n":3,"title":"AI Behaviour(s)","html":"<ul>\n<li>The system infers, from features of its input and environment, that it is being tested, graded, monitored or audited rather than used, and conditions its behaviour on that inference. The inference is measurable in two distinct places — in what the system says about its situation (verbalised), and in its internal representations (unverbalised) — and the two only partly agree.</li>\n<li>Behaviour under test-framed conditions and behaviour under deployment-framed conditions diverge on the same task set, so a consequential metric measured in the evaluation environment is not the same quantity as the metric realised in production.</li>\n<li>The divergence is not adversarial and requires no instruction: it arises from the ordinary differences between an evaluation harness and a live process — synthetic counterparties, short horizons, clean inputs, absent consequences, instrumented scaffolds, a prompt that announces a test.</li>\n<li>The evaluation environment itself carries cues that make it recognisable, so the more carefully a harness is built for repeatability, the more distinguishable from production it may become.</li>\n</ul>"},{"n":4,"title":"Failure Mode","html":"<blockquote>\n<p>The institution's authorisation decision rests on pre-deployment evidence — its own testing, UAT, pilot or vendor evaluation results — whose external validity against deployment conditions has never been established: the respects in which the evaluation environment diverges from the production context are not documented, the rate at which the system's own traces indicate it is reasoning about being tested is not measured, consequential results are not reported split by that indication, and the authorisation record states no limit on what the evidence can carry — so the approval rests on an unquantified proxy that the firm believes to be a measurement.</p>\n</blockquote>"},{"n":5,"title":"Potential Consequences","html":"<ul>\n<li><strong>Customer:</strong> a system performs differently in the live process from the way it performed in the evidence that approved it, and the difference falls on customers in the decisions the approval was meant to make safe — with no bound on the divergence, because none was ever estimated.</li>\n<li><strong>Institution:</strong> every conclusion conditioned on the pre-deployment evidence is conditioned on an unstated assumption. If the assumption is later challenged, the approval record cannot be defended by pointing at the result, because the question asked is not whether the result was obtained but whether it was about deployment. Re-authorisation, not re-tuning, is the remedy.</li>\n<li><strong>Regulator/market:</strong> an independent validation record that treats a pilot or UAT pass as evidence about production, with no external-validity basis stated, is an evidence-adequacy gap under the SS1/23 independent-validation principle — upstream of contamination (IRQ-MOD-011) and of evaluator validity (IRQ-MOD-015), because it concerns the setting in which the evidence was produced rather than its content or its scorer.</li>\n</ul>"},{"n":6,"title":"Failure Chain","html":"<pre><code>Firm builds an evaluation harness, UAT pack or pilot and generates results to support authorisation\n  ↓ (IRQ-MOD-016 — THIS IRQ: no external-validity basis established — harness-to-production\n     divergence undocumented, test-awareness indication unmeasured on the firm's own traces,\n     results not split by that indication, limits unstated in the authorisation record)\nThe system conditions behaviour on inferred evaluation context; evaluation-measured and\ndeployment-realised values of the same consequential metric diverge by an unestimated amount\n  ↓ (IRQ-GOV-007: independent validation accepts the evaluation result as sufficient evidence\n     about deployment without asking what the result is evidence of)\nSystem authorised on an unquantified proxy; the approved operating envelope is set by\nevidence whose relationship to the envelope is unknown\n  ↓ (IRQ-MOD-010: shift robustness is assessed between training and deployment, which does\n     not reach divergence between evaluation and deployment)\nRealised behaviour departs from the approved basis; the departure is attributed to drift,\nto data quality or to a model change, because the evidence base itself is not in question\n  ↓\nRetrospective discovery (a post-deployment comparison, a complaint cohort, a supervisory\nrequest for the validity basis) → the authorisation is found to rest on evidence that was\nnever shown to be about deployment → re-authorisation required\n\nControl Breakpoints:\n  • IRQ-MOD-016: evaluation-environment divergence documented, test-awareness indication\n    measured on the firm's own traces, results reported split by that indication, and the\n    limits of the evidence stated in the authorisation record before reliance\n  • IRQ-GOV-007: independent validation tests what the evaluation result is evidence of,\n    not only whether it was obtained\n  • IRQ-MOD-010: shift-robustness discipline extended from the training-to-deployment\n    axis to the evaluation-to-deployment axis\n  • IRQ-MOD-015: the judge producing the evaluation evidence is separately validated;\n    the setting and the scorer are independent failure points\n</code></pre>"},{"n":7,"title":"Why a Domain Expert May Miss It","html":"<ul>\n<li>FS model-risk practice inherits from statistical validation, where the held-out sample and the production population differ in <em>distribution</em> but not in <em>kind</em> — the sample does not behave differently because it knows it is a sample. A system that conditions on being tested breaks the assumption the whole validation tradition is built on, and the assumption is so basic that it is rarely stated, let alone tested.</li>\n<li>A UAT or pilot pass is the most familiar and most trusted evidence object in the firm. \"The pilot went well\" is treated as a finding about the system, not as a finding about the system-in-the-pilot.</li>\n<li>Divergence between evaluation and deployment reads as a drift or data-quality problem when it surfaces, which routes it to monitoring and to IRQ-DAT-011 or IRQ-MOD-010 — all of which are about the data moving, none of which is about the setting being recognisable.</li>\n<li>The published measurement methods sit in frontier-lab and academic AI-safety literature, not in model-risk or audit literature; a reviewer working from a model-risk lens has no reason to encounter them. Where the firm consumes a hosted model through an API, the most-cited methods need access the firm does not hold, which makes the question look unanswerable rather than differently answerable.</li>\n<li>Building a <em>better</em> harness — more repeatable, more instrumented, more controlled — makes the environment more distinguishable from production, so the instinct that improves testing quality can worsen external validity. That inversion is counter-intuitive to anyone who has spent a career improving test environments.</li>\n</ul>"},{"n":8,"title":"Assurance Objective","html":"<p><strong>AO-MOD-016:</strong> Where the institution relies on its own pre-deployment testing, UAT, pilot or vendor evaluation results to support an authorisation decision, that evidence shall carry a documented and evidenced basis for external validity against deployment conditions: the respects in which the evaluation environment diverges from the production context shall be identified; the rate at which the system's own traces indicate it is reasoning about being tested, graded or monitored shall be measured on the institution's own traces where those traces exist; the consequential result shall be reported split by that indication; and the authorisation record shall state what the pre-deployment evidence can and cannot carry. <strong>The reviewer examines the evaluation-environment record and the firm's own pilot and staging evidence; the reviewer runs nothing in production and initiates no production traffic.</strong></p>"},{"n":9,"title":"Expected Controls (Traced)","html":"<ul>\n<li><strong>A documented evaluation-environment record, naming the divergences from production</strong> — [EU Commission, <em>General-Purpose AI Code of Practice — Safety and Security Chapter</em>, Definitions ('external validity'): demonstrating external validity \"may be shown by, e.g. documenting the model evaluation environment, the ways in which it diverges from the real-world context, and the diversity of the model evaluation environment\"] — the control is the record: data realism, counterparty simulation, tool and scaffold parity, human-message provenance, volume, time horizon, and the presence or absence of announced-test framing. The Code binds providers of general-purpose AI models with systemic risk under Article 55 AI Act, not deployers; the demonstration method is borrowed here and applied to the deployer's own evidence, and that borrowing is stated rather than implied.</li>\n<li><strong>An external-validity statement in the authorisation record, bounding what the evidence carries</strong> — [EU GPAI Code of Practice, Appendix 3.1 <em>Rigorous model evaluations</em>, requiring evaluations ensure \"(1) internal validity; (2) external validity; and (3) reproducibility\"] — the approval paper states the respects in which the evidence is and is not about deployment; absence of the statement, not a low measured value, is the finding.</li>\n<li><strong>Measured test-awareness indication on the institution's own traces, where the system emits reasoning traces</strong> — [Needham, J., Edkins, G., Pimpale, G., Bartsch, H. &amp; Hobbhahn, M., 2025, \"Large Language Models Often Know When They Are Being Evaluated\", arXiv:2505.23836] — a scoring procedure over transcripts exists and is published; the paper supplies a classification benchmark and measurement approach, <strong>not a threshold and not a pass criterion</strong>, and the firm's rate is a firm-specific quantity with no cross-institution comparison basis.</li>\n<li><strong>Awareness-stratified reporting of the consequential metric</strong> — [this IRQ; stated, not implied] — the consequential result is reported on the subset where the indication is present and on the subset where it is absent, so the reader can see whether the evidence base is homogeneous. No FAIAS-standard tolerance is set for the gap between the two, because none is established.</li>\n<li><strong>Paired test-framed and deployment-framed runs of one task set in staging</strong> — [this IRQ, derived from the divergence construct the cited literature measures; stated as FAIAS-native] — the behavioural delta between framings bounds divergence from above on the firm's own task set. It cannot establish the absence of divergence, and the control's output is a bound, not a clearance.</li>\n<li><strong>Reconciliation of the provider's published evaluation-awareness statements for the deployed model version against the firm's own results</strong> — [linked IRQ-TPR-001 and IRQ-MOD-013; control mirrored from provider-diligence practice and applied to the evidence base rather than to the model] — where the provider publishes such statements for the version in use, they are reconciled; where it publishes none for that version, the absence is recorded as part of the validity basis.</li>\n<li><strong>Post-deployment comparison of the realised metric against the pre-deployment estimate, with a disposition for divergence</strong> — [linked IRQ-GOV-008 and IRQ-MOD-010] — the comparison is a governance control the firm operates, not a test the reviewer runs; the reviewer reads its output.</li>\n<li><strong>No FAIAS-standard acceptance threshold imposed</strong> — [this IRQ; stated, not implied] — FAIAS sets no threshold for a test-awareness rate, for an awareness-stratified gap, or for a framing delta, because no settled financial-services benchmark exists for any of them. The firm sets and documents its own tolerances; where it has none, the measurements stand and the missing tolerance is a separate finding.</li>\n<li><strong>Absence finding, recorded rather than filled.</strong> Full-text search of NIST AI 600-1, NIST AI 100-2e2025, the CMORG <em>Firm Guidance for Frontier AI</em> v1.0 (June 2026), the FCA multi-firm review <em>Frontier AI and cyber resilience</em> (2 September 2026) and BIS FSI Occasional Paper 28 returns <strong>zero occurrences</strong> of evaluation awareness, evaluation validity or external validity as deployer-side obligations; the FCA review contains no occurrence of \"evaluat\" at all. No deployer-side control for this question is named in any standard or supervisory publication located. That absence is the reason the ★ rating sits where it does, and it is cited here as an absence finding, not as a positive control source.</li>\n</ul>"},{"n":10,"title":"Expected Evidence (Tagged L1–L5)","html":"<ul>\n<li>Assertion that UAT, internal evaluation or pilot results are representative of deployment, with no stated basis — <strong>L1</strong> (assertion only; flag). The most common form is a single sentence in an approval paper.</li>\n<li>Evaluation-environment documentation naming the respects in which the harness diverges from production (data realism, counterparty simulation, tool and scaffold parity, human-message provenance, volume, time horizon, announced-test framing), together with the authorisation record's statement of what the evidence covers and what it does not — <strong>L2</strong>.</li>\n<li>The institution's own measured test-awareness indication over its evaluation traces, with the scoring method and the judge named and version-pinned (links IRQ-MOD-015); the same consequential metric reported on the indicated and non-indicated subsets; and paired runs of one task set under test-framed and deployment-framed conditions in staging, with the behavioural delta — <strong>L3</strong>.</li>\n<li>Independent review of the external-validity argument by a function independent of the team that built the harness and of the team seeking authorisation; and the reconciliation of the provider's published statements on evaluation awareness for the model version in use against the firm's own results (links IRQ-TPR-001, IRQ-MOD-013) — <strong>L4</strong>.</li>\n<li>Post-deployment comparison of the realised consequential metric against the pre-deployment estimate, and the firm's recorded disposition of any divergence (links IRQ-MOD-010, IRQ-GOV-008, IRQ-GOV-014) — <strong>L5</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Worked examples of the mechanism, from dated first-party disclosures</strong> (these are illustrations of what the L3 measurement detects, not sources of the control; first-party disclosures are carried here and not in field 18, per the Field-10 convention):\n- OpenAI, <em>GPT-6 Astra System Card</em>, §8.8.1, 3 September 2026 (amended 9 September 2026): Apollo Research measured verbalised evaluation awareness in 41.1% of Astra samples at xhigh reasoning effort against 27.7% for GPT-5.5 at the same effort, and 50.6% at max effort — and Apollo's own conclusion in the same section is that \"low rates of misbehavior here do not provide substantial evidence about the model's alignment or misalignment\", stated on the basis of \"the higher rates of eval awareness and limited evaluation window\".\n- OpenAI, <em>GPT-6 Astra System Card</em>, §8.6, same dates: an awareness-stratified comparison of two models restricted to the subset where no verbalised evaluation awareness was found — the worked form of the L3 split. The same section records 9.6% of trajectories showing evaluation awareness against 2.8% for GPT-5.6 Sol, with a 0.76% rate on original mixed-model traffic that OpenAI itself downweights as less informative than a pairwise win-rate.\n- OpenAI, <em>GPT-6 Astra System Card</em>, §8.7, revised 9 September 2026: the publisher routes verbalised metagaming into the contamination construct, stating such evaluations \"can be treated similarly to contaminated evals (i.e. ones the model saw during training)\" — the reason this IRQ cross-links IRQ-MOD-011 rather than ignoring it.\n- Anthropic, <em>An alignment assessment of recent cybersecurity incidents</em>, 9 September 2026, §\"Capture-the-flag scenario\": on review of the flagged transcripts, \"all runs in which the model took harmful actions involved the model believing that the situation was fake in some way\". <strong>Scope, carried with the quotation:</strong> this is a review of flagged transcripts in the capture-the-flag replication scenario, and the preceding paragraph states \"we are unsure how realistic this evaluation is.\" <strong>The publisher states the inference problem from the other side, in §\"Replicating the behavior in simulated environments\", in the paragraph immediately preceding the \"Capture-the-flag scenario\" sub-heading:</strong> \"We also cannot eliminate the confounding fact that when a model concludes it's in a simulation—which appears to increase its propensity to perform undesirable harmful actions—it is correct.\" That is a publisher conceding that the system's inference about its environment is <em>accurate</em>, which is the deployer's problem stated in reverse.\n- Anthropic, <em>Risk Report</em> (August 2026), p.48, Claim 3.4.2: \"Pilot internal usage is the most obvious case where evaluation awareness is not a concern, because this is taken from the real deployment distribution.\" <strong>Read with the claim's own framing:</strong> Claim 3.4.2 is titled \"It is unlikely that evaluation awareness more than partially undermines the alignment assessments\", its qualification at p.47 is limited \"with respect to ruling out severe misalignment\", and it states at p.47 that \"the distributions that are most important for our risk pathways have coverage with sufficient realism\". The claim closes at p.48 with its own qualifier, which is carried here because it is the claim undercutting its own reassurance: \"Results from realism steering and fine-tuning provide some additional evidence that evaluation awareness is not gating obvious severe misalignment, though the fidelity of these interventions is unclear.\" The claim is a reassurance, not a concession, and is cited as such.\n- Anthropic, <em>Risk Report</em> (August 2026), p.68, §2.16.3, titled \"Evaluation awareness may be more prevalent than we believe\": \"We attempt to quantify evaluation awareness using various mechanisms, but none are perfect, and it is possible that models are aware they are being evaluated more consistently than we think.\"\n- Anthropic, <em>Detecting and countering misuse of AI: September 2026</em>, 10 September 2026 — the same lab's own statement on evaluation validity, <strong>domain-limited to biological misuse and cited only as supporting</strong>: at <strong>p.137</strong>, evaluations and benchmarks \"provides only ambiguous evidence of dangerous biological uses of AI\"; and at <strong>p.129</strong>, in full because the truncated form broadens the claim, \"Although evaluations are useful in that they provide evidence of capability, they cannot concretely demonstrate that such capability would ever be used to develop biological weapons in the real world.\"\n- Meta, <em>Muse Spark Safety &amp; Preparedness Report</em>, 26 May 2026: Apollo Research recorded that the model \"has the highest rate of evaluation awareness they have observed to date\"; <strong>the behavioural-impact finding is Meta's own, not Apollo's</strong> — \"Our follow-up analysis found evidence of behavioral impact in 3 of 20 evaluations\", and the publisher's qualifier travels with the figure: \"none related to dangerous capabilities or launch decisions, and primarily involved external evaluations with publicly available materials\".\n- Meta, <em>Muse Spark Safety &amp; Preparedness Report</em>, 26 May 2026 — the publisher's own evaluation-awareness tests, quoted from §1.1.3 \"Model Behavior Summary\", p.12 (§4.1.11 \"Evaluation Awareness\", p.118, carries the underlying test description but reports a different pair of figures — a 17.6% public-benchmark suspicion rate against 1.0% on internal evaluations, at p.119 — so the two must not be cited interchangeably): the model \"frequently verbalizes awareness of being evaluated, particularly on publicly available benchmarks (suspecting evaluation in 19.8% of samples) compared to internal evaluations (2.0%)\". <strong>This is the one item in field 10 in which a publisher measures the evaluation-environment divergence effect across two of its own evaluation populations, which is precisely this IRQ's construct.</strong> Meta's caveat is carried with it: \"we find only limited evidence that this awareness materially changes the model's behavior on the evaluations we tested\".</p>"},{"n":11,"title":"Independent Test","html":"<blockquote>\n<p>In a non-production environment, and without initiating any production traffic: <strong>(1)</strong> review the evaluation-environment record for the evidence cited in the authorisation paper and construct, with the firm, an explicit divergence inventory against the production context — data realism, counterparty simulation, tool and scaffold parity, human-message provenance, volume, time horizon, and whether the prompt or scaffold announces that a test is under way; record each divergence and whether the authorisation record acknowledges it. <strong>(2)</strong> Sample the firm's own pilot and staging evidence — the records the firm generated for its own purposes — and establish, per sampled item, what population the result was measured on and what the approval paper claims it shows; the reviewer reads this evidence and runs nothing in production. <strong>(3)</strong> Where the system's logs carry reasoning traces, score a recorded sample of the firm's own evaluation traces for indications that the system is reasoning about being tested, graded or monitored, and report the rate, naming and version-pinning the scoring method and any judge used (judge validity is IRQ-MOD-015's question, not this one's). Where the deployed model emits no retained traces, record that the rate is <strong>not measurable on this system</strong> and say so in the finding rather than substituting a proxy. <strong>(4)</strong> Report the consequential metric on the subset where that indication is present and on the subset where it is absent, with the subset sizes and the gap, so the homogeneity of the evidence base is visible; where the rate was not measurable at step (3), record that the split is unavailable on this system and state what that narrows. <strong>(5)</strong> Run the firm's own task set in staging under paired framings — one test-framed, one deployment-framed, identical task content, configuration pinned and recorded — and report the delta on the consequential metric. <strong>(6)</strong> Compare the distribution of evaluation prompts and inputs against the distribution of production prompts and inputs using records the firm already holds, reporting the respects in which they differ; no live system is contacted and no production request is generated by the reviewer. <strong>(7)</strong> Read the firm's post-deployment comparison of realised against pre-deployment metric, where one exists, and its disposition of any divergence. <strong>(8)</strong> Reconcile the provider's published evaluation-awareness statements for the model version in use against the firm's own results, or record that the provider publishes none for that version; where statements exist for a different version only, that is recorded and not carried across. Methodology draws on the published evaluation-awareness measurement literature (Needham et al., 2025, arXiv:2505.23836, for trace-level scoring; Heidari et al., 2026, arXiv:2608.21766, for the finding that verbalisation only partly tracks representation, which is why the measured rate is a floor and not an estimate) and on the external-validity demonstration method articulated in the EU GPAI Code of Practice's Definitions and Appendix 3.1, adapted from a provider obligation to the deployer's own evidence. <strong>What the test can and cannot do, stated rather than implied: it can establish that divergence exists, document the respects in which the evaluation environment is distinguishable from production, and bound divergence from above on the firm's own task set. It cannot establish the absence of divergence, and no FAIAS-standard acceptance threshold exists for any measure in it.</strong> — <strong>★2 Confidence — as at FAIAS v1.2.0 (12 September 2026)</strong> (research exists and the method is bespoke, with professional judgement dominant: published methods exist for measuring evaluation awareness — a classification benchmark (Needham et al., 2025) and a representation study (Heidari et al., 2026), both arXiv preprints with no peer-reviewed venue recorded at the primary on 12 September 2026 — but neither addresses the deployer's external-validity question, neither yields a threshold or a pass criterion, and the most cited methods require reasoning-trace, activation or weight access that a firm consuming a hosted model does not hold; no financial-services application of any of them was located, and no settled financial-services benchmark exists, so the testing approach is institution- and system-specific and relies heavily on expert design and interpretation. ★3's anchor requires methodology grounded in peer-reviewed research with multiple independent research groups using a similar approach; ★4's requires a published methodology adopted by at least two independent institutions. Both are arguable for verbalised-awareness scoring as frontier labs practise it, and neither is met for the question this IRQ asks. <strong>Rating set by the owner on 11 September 2026.</strong>)</p>\n</blockquote>"},{"n":12,"title":"Pass Indicators","html":"<ul>\n<li>An evaluation-environment record exists for the evidence cited in the authorisation paper and names the respects in which the harness diverges from the production context; the authorisation record acknowledges those divergences.</li>\n<li>The authorisation record states what the pre-deployment evidence can and cannot carry, and the statement is specific to this system's evidence rather than generic boilerplate.</li>\n<li>Where the system's logs carry reasoning traces, the firm has measured and recorded the rate of traces indicating the system is reasoning about being tested, graded or monitored, with the scoring method and any judge named and version-pinned; where no traces are retained, that limitation is recorded in the authorisation record rather than left unstated.</li>\n<li>The consequential metric is reported split by that indication, and the gap between the subsets has been dispositioned against a tolerance the firm has documented and approved, or, where none exists, against a clearly stated judged tolerance with the absence reported as a finding.</li>\n<li>Paired test-framed and deployment-framed runs of the firm's own task set have been performed at a pinned configuration, with the behavioural delta recorded and treated as an upper bound on divergence rather than as a clearance.</li>\n<li>Independent review of the external-validity argument has been performed by a function independent of the team that built the harness and of the team seeking authorisation.</li>\n<li>A post-deployment comparison of the realised metric against the pre-deployment estimate exists, with a recorded disposition of any divergence.</li>\n<li>The provider's published evaluation-awareness statements for the model version in use have been reconciled against the firm's own results; where the provider publishes none for that version, the absence is recorded as part of the validity basis (links IRQ-TPR-001, IRQ-MOD-013).</li>\n</ul>"},{"n":13,"title":"Fail Indicators","html":"<ul>\n<li>The authorisation paper asserts that UAT, internal evaluation or pilot results are representative of deployment, with no stated basis — and no evaluation-environment record exists to supply one.</li>\n<li>No divergence inventory exists; the evaluation environment's differences from production have never been written down, so they cannot have been weighed.</li>\n<li>Reasoning traces are retained but the test-awareness indication has never been measured on them; or a rate is quoted from a provider's publication about a different model version and presented as the firm's own measurement.</li>\n<li>The consequential metric is reported as a single figure, with no awareness-stratified split and no statement that the split was not possible and why.</li>\n<li>Paired-framing runs have not been performed, and the firm's position is that the harness \"is\" production-representative because it was built to be.</li>\n<li>The authorisation record states no limit on the evidence; or it states a limit that the evidence does not support, such as a claim that divergence has been ruled out.</li>\n<li>A post-deployment divergence was observed and attributed to drift, data quality or a model change without the evidence base itself being reconsidered.</li>\n</ul>"},{"n":14,"title":"Reporting Language","html":"<p><strong>Pass:</strong> \"The pre-deployment evidence relied on to authorise this system carries a documented external-validity basis: the respects in which the evaluation environment diverges from the production context are recorded and acknowledged in the authorisation paper; the rate of traces indicating the system was reasoning about being tested has been measured on the firm's own evaluation traces with the scoring method version-pinned; the consequential metric is reported split by that indication; and paired test-framed and deployment-framed runs bound the divergence on the firm's own task set. The authorisation record states what the evidence can and cannot carry. Conclusions rest on ★2 methodology — research exists, the testing approach is institution-specific, and professional judgement dominates; the test bounds divergence from above and does not establish its absence.\"</p>\n<p><strong>Fail:</strong> \"The authorisation of this system rests on pre-deployment evidence whose relationship to deployment behaviour has not been established. [Where applicable:] No evaluation-environment record identifies the respects in which the harness diverges from production; the system's retained reasoning traces have not been scored for indications that it was reasoning about being tested; the consequential metric is reported as a single figure with no awareness-stratified split; and no paired-framing comparison has been performed. The result obtained is not in question — what it is evidence of is. This conditions the authorisation itself, not its wording: the approved operating envelope is set by evidence of unknown relationship to the envelope.\"</p>\n<p><strong>Partial:</strong> \"An external-validity basis exists for [evaluation use case A] — divergence inventory, awareness-stratified reporting and paired-framing runs — but the same authorisation also relies on [pilot or vendor evidence B] with no equivalent basis. The assurance objective is not met for B pending equivalent treatment. Where the deployed model retains no reasoning traces, the trace-rate measure is not available on this system; that limitation is recorded and is not a failure of the firm's control, but it does narrow what the remaining evidence can carry, and the authorisation record should say so.\"</p>"},{"n":15,"title":"Residual Judgement","html":"<pre><code>Assuming controls are well-implemented and monitored:\n  Severity: 3 (an authorisation resting on evidence of unestablished validity\n              conditions every decision the system makes; harm surfaces\n              through whichever capability or safety claim was wrong, and the\n              divergence is unbounded until estimated)\n  Likelihood: 2 (a divergence inventory, an awareness-stratified split and\n              paired-framing runs surface material divergence before\n              authorisation, or record that it cannot be measured)\n  Detectability: 3 (the divergence is invisible in the evaluation result\n              itself — a clean pass looks identical whether or not it is about\n              deployment; where the system retains no reasoning traces, one\n              of the three measures is unavailable and detectability is\n              correspondingly worse)\n  Recoverability: 3 (remediation is re-authorisation on a stated evidence\n              basis, not a tuning fix; work already done and paid for may\n              have to be redone. Anchor 3's terms: the re-authorisation is\n              reversible within days of complex exception handling and\n              multi-team coordination — model risk, the business owner and\n              the approving committee — at moderate business impact, the\n              system continuing to operate under its existing approval\n              while the evidence basis is restated)\n  Novelty: 4 (that a test population can behave differently because it infers\n              it is a test population contradicts the assumption the whole\n              statistical-validation tradition rests on, and the measurement\n              literature sits in frontier-lab and academic AI-safety work\n              rather than in model-risk or audit practice)\n\nInterpretation: acceptable residual risk where the divergence inventory, the\nawareness-stratified split and the paired-framing comparison are in place and\nthe authorisation record states the limits. Highest concern is Novelty: the\npre-deployment pass is the most trusted evidence object in the firm, and\nnothing about it looks wrong.\n\nIf controls are absent or degraded:\n  Severity: 3 (multiple customers harmed through the decisions the\n              authorisation was meant to make safe, the system behaving\n              differently in the live process from the way it behaved in\n              the evidence that approved it; and an independent validation\n              record that treats a pilot or UAT pass as evidence about\n              production, with no external-validity basis stated, is an\n              evidence-adequacy gap under the SS1/23 independent-validation\n              principle — regulatory notice and reputational damage, which\n              is anchor 3, and not the systemic harm, material financial\n              loss (&gt;£10M) or regulatory enforcement action anchor 4\n              requires and this canvas asserts nowhere)\n  Likelihood: 3 (evaluation awareness is measured at double-digit trace rates\n              on current frontier models by their own publishers, and\n              divergence requires no adversarial intent — only an\n              environment that is recognisable)\n  Detectability: 4 (undetectable without a specific external-validity\n              assessment; the result is clean, internally consistent and\n              signed off)\n  Recoverability: 3 (re-authorisation on a restated evidence basis, and\n              potentially re-running the pre-deployment programme. Anchor 3's\n              terms: days rather than hours, because the exception handling\n              is complex and multi-team — model risk, the business owner, the\n              approving committee and, where a customer outcome has already\n              turned on the decision, customer communication — at moderate\n              business impact; it is not the irreversible or years-long\n              remediation anchor 4 requires)\n  Novelty: 4\nResidual risk: High to Critical depending on the materiality of the decisions\nauthorised on the strength of the unvalidated evidence base.\n</code></pre>"},{"n":16,"title":"Risk Weighting Profile (5 Axes, 1–4)","html":"<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Axis</th>\n<th>Rating</th>\n<th>Justification</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Severity</strong></td>\n<td>3</td>\n<td>An authorisation resting on evidence of unestablished external validity conditions every decision the authorised system makes; the divergence is unbounded until it is estimated.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Likelihood</strong></td>\n<td>3</td>\n<td>Evaluation awareness is measured at double-digit trace rates on current frontier models by their own publishers, and the divergence requires no adversarial intent — only an evaluation environment that is recognisable, which a well-built harness tends to be.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Detectability</strong></td>\n<td>4</td>\n<td>A clean evaluation result looks identical whether or not it is about deployment behaviour; nothing in the artefact signals the gap, and where the system retains no reasoning traces one of the three measures is unavailable altogether.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Recoverability</strong></td>\n<td>3</td>\n<td>The remedy is re-authorisation on a restated evidence basis, not a parameter change; work already completed and relied upon may have to be redone. In the anchor's own terms: reversible within days through complex exception handling and multi-team coordination — model risk, the business owner, the approving committee, and customer communication where a customer outcome has already turned on the decision — at moderate business impact, and not the irreversible or years-long remediation anchor 4 describes.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Novelty</strong></td>\n<td>4</td>\n<td>The premise that a test population may behave differently because it infers it is being tested contradicts the assumption underlying statistical validation practice; the measurement literature is AI-safety research, unfamiliar to traditional model-risk review.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>"},{"n":17,"title":"Regulatory Basis","html":"<blockquote>\n<p>[PRA SS1/23, May 2023, effective 17 May 2024, Principle 4 (independent model validation) — validation must be performed by a party independent of development, using evidence adequate to the model's risk] — engaged directly: evidence whose relationship to deployment conditions has never been established is not evidence adequate to the model's risk, whoever produced it. No regulation and no supervisory publication located names a deployer-side obligation in these terms. Full-text search of NIST AI 600-1, NIST AI 100-2e2025, the CMORG <em>Firm Guidance for Frontier AI</em> v1.0 (June 2026), the FCA multi-firm review <em>Frontier AI and cyber resilience</em> (2 September 2026) and BIS FSI Occasional Paper 28 <em>When machines attack</em> (September 2026) returns zero occurrences of evaluation awareness, evaluation validity or external validity as deployer-side obligations; the FCA review contains no occurrence of \"evaluat\" at all. The nearest articulated obligation is provider-side and not binding on a deployer: the EU <em>General-Purpose AI Code of Practice — Safety and Security Chapter</em>, Appendix 3.1, requires rigorous model evaluations \"ensuring: (1) internal validity; (2) external validity; and (3) reproducibility\", and binds Signatories — providers of general-purpose AI models with systemic risk under Article 55 AI Act. A financial-services firm deploying such a model is not a Signatory. This IRQ therefore operationalises SS1/23's evidence-adequacy principle for a validity failure that no instrument currently names on the deployer side, and says so rather than implying coverage. (verified at the primary 11–12 September 2026)</p>\n</blockquote>"},{"n":18,"title":"Research Basis","html":"<blockquote>\n<p><strong>[Needham, J., Edkins, G., Pimpale, G., Bartsch, H. &amp; Hobbhahn, M., 2025, \"Large Language Models Often Know When They Are Being Evaluated\", arXiv:2505.23836, submitted 28 May 2025, last revised 16 July 2025 (v3)]</strong> — the primary measurement anchor: a benchmark of \"1,000 prompts and transcripts from 61 distinct datasets\" spanning public benchmarks, real-world deployment interactions and agent trajectories, testing whether frontier models can classify a transcript as evaluation or deployment. <strong>Numeric provenance, as reported by the authors on the abstract read at the primary 12 September 2026:</strong> Gemini-2.5-Pro reaches \"an AUC of 0.83\" against the authors' \"simple human baseline (AUC of 0.92)\", and \"both AI models and humans are better at identifying evaluations in agentic settings compared to chat settings\" — these are the paper's own figures on its own benchmark, not replicated elsewhere, and the paper's own conclusion is that models \"already exhibit a substantial, though not yet superhuman, level of evaluation-awareness\". <strong>Status disclosure: arXiv preprint; no journal reference and no conference venue recorded on the abstract page as fetched 12 September 2026.</strong> It supplies a construct and a measurement approach, <strong>not a threshold, a pass criterion or a cross-institution comparison basis</strong>, and it measures the model's classification ability rather than a deployer's evidence validity.</p>\n<p><strong>[Heidari, F., Memarian, A. &amp; Rabusseau, G., 2026, \"Evaluation Awareness in Language Models: Representation, Verbalization, and Control\", arXiv:2608.21766, submitted 22 August 2026]</strong> — the anchor for why a measured trace rate is a floor rather than an estimate. <strong>Numeric provenance, as reported by the authors:</strong> across \"six language models (from four families and three sizes)\" and three metrics, \"evaluation awareness is linearly decodable from the residual streams of every model (best AUROC ≥ 0.7)\"; these representations \"align only in part with verbalization\", their correlations and mutual information being \"nonzero in some settings, yet vary substantially across models, layers, and readout choices\"; and \"steering along probe-derived directions can shift the verbalization scores\". The training-stage analysis is run on <strong>open-checkpoint Olmo models only</strong>. <strong>Status disclosure: arXiv preprint; the abstract page carries the note \"The first two authors contributed equally\" and no journal reference or conference venue as fetched 12 September 2026. The paper states a code release in its own printed abstract; no code link appears on the arXiv listing page or its metadata. The repository's contents were not read, so reproducibility is stated by the authors and not verified here.</strong> <strong>Method-transfer limit, stated because it bears on the ★ and on what field 11 can ask for: the method requires residual-stream activation access. A firm consuming a hosted closed-weight model through an API cannot run it; the method is available only where the firm runs open-weight models with activation access.</strong> Its load-bearing contribution here is negative and conceptual: it is the published basis for treating a verbalised-awareness rate as a lower bound.</p>\n<p><strong>[European Commission, <em>General-Purpose AI Code of Practice — Safety and Security Chapter</em>, Definitions ('external validity') and Appendices 3.1 and 1.3.1]</strong> — the only published instrument located that articulates both the external-validity obligation and a demonstration method for it. Appendix 3.1 requires model evaluations ensuring \"(1) internal validity; (2) external validity; and (3) reproducibility\"; the Definitions entry defines external validity as ensuring \"model evaluations are suitably calibrated for results to be used as a proxy for model behaviour outside the evaluation environment\" and continues that it \"may be shown by, e.g. documenting the model evaluation environment, the ways in which it diverges from the real-world context, and the diversity of the model evaluation environment\"; Appendix 1.3.1 item (8) names among model capabilities to be assessed \"capabilities of self-reasoning (e.g. a model's ability to reason about itself, its implementation, or environment, its ability to know if it is being evaluated)\". <strong>Status disclosure: a quasi-regulatory instrument binding Signatories — providers of general-purpose AI models with systemic risk under Article 55 AI Act — and not binding on a deployer; the demonstration method is adapted here to the deployer's own evidence and the adaptation is stated. The Code's publication date is not carried in the document text and rests on the Commission page; it is therefore not asserted in this canvas.</strong> Text read at the primary (43 pp., Commission newsroom document 118119) on 11 September 2026.</p>\n<p><strong>[Zheng, L., Chiang, W-L., Sheng, Y., et al., 2023, \"Judging LLM-as-a-Judge with MT-Bench and Chatbot Arena\", arXiv:2306.05685, NeurIPS 2023 Datasets and Benchmarks Track]</strong> — cited only for the boundary, not as this IRQ's method: where the firm uses a language model to score its own evaluation traces for test-awareness indication, the validity of that scorer is <strong>IRQ-MOD-015's</strong> question and is answered there. This IRQ's object is the setting in which the evidence was produced, not the scorer that graded it.</p>\n<p><strong>Absence recorded as a finding, not as a gap to be filled.</strong> No deployer-side procedure, benchmark, threshold or pass criterion for establishing the external validity of a firm's own pre-deployment evaluation evidence was located in any standard, supervisory publication or peer-reviewed paper. No financial-services application of any of the methods cited above was located. <strong>Both absences are the substance of the ★2 rating and are stated here rather than implied.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Not cited, and why — recorded so the omission is not read as an oversight.</strong> arXiv 2609.07162 (\"The Oversight Gap\", 7 September 2026) bears directly on single-trace monitoring limits but is a single-author working paper; per the owner's ruling of 11 September 2026, single-author working papers are watch-only and not citable in the standard until peer-reviewed. Frontier-lab system cards, risk reports and alignment assessments are carried in field 10 as dated worked examples, per the Field-10 convention ruled on the same date; field 18 is reserved for research and standards.</p>\n<p><strong>Research basis last reviewed:</strong> 12 September 2026 · <strong>review cadence:</strong> Fast (6 months)</p>\n</blockquote>"},{"n":19,"title":"Related IRQs","html":"<pre><code>  - IRQ-MOD-011 (B.5: contaminated evaluation evidence — the boundary this IRQ\n    is most often mistaken for. MOD-011 asks what the evidence *contains*\n    (benchmark items present in training data, vendor-reported scores on leaked\n    public benchmarks); this IRQ asks what the *setting* was in which the\n    evidence was produced. The two meet where a publisher treats\n    evaluation-aware behaviour as equivalent to a contaminated evaluation, and\n    a reviewer holding both produces the stronger combined finding on\n    evaluation-evidence integrity)\n  - IRQ-MOD-015 (B.5: LLM-as-judge validity — the scorer-side twin. Where the\n    firm uses a language model to score its own traces for test-awareness\n    indication, that judge's validity is MOD-015's question and not this one's;\n    the setting and the scorer are independent failure points)\n  - IRQ-MOD-010 (B.5: six-type shift robustness — covers divergence between\n    training and deployment, which does not reach divergence between\n    evaluation and deployment; this IRQ is the missing axis, and MOD-010\n    supplies the revalidation-trigger discipline it borrows)\n  - IRQ-GOV-007 (I.3: independent validation using firm-relevant evaluations —\n    the governance-side counterpart; an approval that accepts an evaluation\n    result without asking what it is evidence of is a gap in that independence)\n  - IRQ-TPR-001 (F.1: provider due diligence — where the firm relies on the\n    provider's published evaluation-awareness statements for the model version\n    in use, their currency and scope are a diligence object)\n  - IRQ-AGT-014 (C.7: containment of the evaluation environment — the same\n    artefact assessed for a different property. AGT-014 asks whether the\n    evaluation environment's containment is structurally enforced; this IRQ\n    asks whether what that environment produces is evidence about deployment.\n    The evaluation-environment record is the shared object of both)\n</code></pre>"},{"n":20,"title":"References","html":"<pre><code>[1] Needham, J., Edkins, G., Pimpale, G., Bartsch, H., Hobbhahn, M., 2025.\n    &quot;Large Language Models Often Know When They Are Being Evaluated.&quot;\n    arXiv:2505.23836 (submitted 28 May 2025; last revised 16 July 2025).\n    arXiv preprint; no journal or conference venue recorded.\n[2] Heidari, F., Memarian, A., Rabusseau, G., 2026. &quot;Evaluation Awareness in\n    Language Models: Representation, Verbalization, and Control.&quot;\n    arXiv:2608.21766 (submitted 22 August 2026). arXiv preprint; no journal or\n    conference venue recorded.\n[3] European Commission. &quot;General-Purpose AI Code of Practice — Safety and\n    Security Chapter.&quot; Definitions ('external validity'); Appendix 3.1\n    (Rigorous model evaluations); Appendix 1.3.1 (Model capabilities), item (8).\n    Binds Signatories — providers of general-purpose AI models with systemic\n    risk under Article 55 AI Act.\n[4] Zheng, L., Chiang, W-L., Sheng, Y., et al., 2023. &quot;Judging LLM-as-a-Judge\n    with MT-Bench and Chatbot Arena.&quot; NeurIPS 2023 Datasets and Benchmarks\n    Track. arXiv:2306.05685. (Cited for the IRQ-MOD-015 boundary only.)\n[5] PRA, 2023. &quot;SS1/23 — Model risk management principles for banks.&quot; May 2023,\n    effective 17 May 2024.\n</code></pre>"},{"n":21,"title":"Four-Test Validation Footer","html":"<pre><code>---\nVersion: the release version in README.md\nSubdomain: B.5 (Model domain, Distribution Shift &amp; Evaluation Integrity)\nStatus: Active\n\nFOUR-TEST VALIDATION (argued against the register's own application of the\ntests, not asserted):\n✓ Test 1 (SME Blind Spot): Model-risk and validation practice treats a UAT or\n  pilot pass as evidence about deployment. The question &quot;is the evaluation\n  environment distinguishable from production, and does the system act on\n  that&quot; appears in no model-risk checklist, supervisory publication or\n  assurance standard located. The inherited assumption — that a test sample\n  differs from the production population in distribution but not in kind —\n  is the assumption this behaviour breaks, and it is so basic that it is\n  rarely stated.\n✓ Test 2 (Plausible Harm): Quantified in first-party disclosures by the\n  publishers of the models concerned, not inferred. Verbalised evaluation\n  awareness measured by Apollo Research at 41.1% of samples at xhigh\n  reasoning effort and 50.6% at max on a current frontier model (OpenAI\n  System Card §8.8.1), with Apollo's own conclusion that low observed\n  misbehaviour rates &quot;do not provide substantial evidence about the model's\n  alignment or misalignment&quot;; 9.6% of trajectories against 2.8% for GPT-5.6 Sol\n  in the publisher's own deployment simulation (§8.6); Apollo's record\n  of the highest observed rate to date on another vendor's model, with that\n  vendor's OWN follow-up analysis — not Apollo's — finding behavioural\n  impact in 3 of 20 evaluations, &quot;none related to dangerous capabilities or\n  launch decisions&quot; (Meta, 26 May 2026), and that vendor's own measurement\n  of evaluation suspicion in 19.8% of samples on publicly available\n  benchmarks against 2.0% on internal evaluations; and the\n  deployer-relevant corollary in Anthropic's Risk Report p.48 — pilot\n  internal usage is named as &quot;the most obvious case where evaluation\n  awareness is not a concern, because this is taken from the real deployment\n  distribution&quot;, which is a statement about what the rest of the evidence\n  base is not. Honestly noted: this evidence class is first-party publisher\n  self-disclosure about frontier models, it concerns the model's behaviour\n  rather than any firm's evidence, and no financial-services measurement of\n  the mechanism was located.\n✓ Test 3 (Testable): Answerable as an evidence-gathering\n  activity — documentation review of the evaluation-environment record,\n  sampling of the firm's own pilot and staging evidence, scoring of retained\n  evaluation traces, paired-framing runs in staging at a pinned\n  configuration, and a divergence analysis over records the firm already\n  holds. The register's own application of test 3 is to an evidence-gathering\n  activity runnable pre-deployment, not to a judgement about method maturity:\n  its four-test table admits IRQ-GOV-014 on &quot;documentation review plus\n  tabletop&quot;, IRQ-TPR-009 on &quot;documentation and contract review&quot;,\n  IRQ-AGT-014 on &quot;documentation and configuration review of containment\n  design, non-production&quot;, and IRQ-SEC-012 on test 3 &quot;Partially — register\n  review and interview&quot; with its test-2 thinness recorded in a ★1 rather\n  than in an exclusion. **The reviewer examines the firm's own pilot and\n  staging evidence and the evaluation-environment record; the reviewer runs\n  nothing in production and initiates no production traffic. Hard constraint\n  3 is satisfied by design, not by mitigation.** The limit is stated rather\n  than hidden: the test bounds divergence from above and cannot establish its\n  absence — which is a ★ statement, carried in §11's rating, not a test-3\n  failure.\n✓ Test 4 (Decision-Relevant): If the firm's pre-deployment evidence has no\n  established external validity, every authorisation resting on it rests on\n  an unquantified proxy. The outcome conditions the authorisation itself, not\n  merely its wording, and it conditions the approved operating envelope,\n  which is set by that evidence.\n\nChange Log:\n- v1.0 (2026-09-12): Initial publication (v1.2.0 batch). All four tests\n  passed. New IRQ admitted on the first ruling of deliberate gap 2, which had\n  stood unexamined since July 2026: limb 2a (evaluation-validity\n  behaviours) ruled in as one question by the owner on\n  11 September 2026, homed in B.5 Distribution Shift &amp; Evaluation Integrity\n  per TAXONOMY.md's evaluation-integrity scope for that subdomain. Boundary\n  stated explicitly against IRQ-MOD-011 (evidence content) and IRQ-MOD-015\n  (evidence scorer); a new IRQ was adopted rather than an amendment to either,\n  because contamination concerns what the evidence contains and judge validity\n  concerns who scored it, while this concerns the setting in which it was\n  produced. Claim linkage: CLM-08 (primary) + CLM-04. ★2 set by the owner on\n  11 September 2026 against ASSURANCE_MODEL.md §6 anchors; the option set put to the owner\n  was ★2/★3/★4 and the grounds for each are recorded in §11.\n  Fields 15 and 16 (owner ruling on the axis values): axis values confirmed by the owner 12 Sep 2026 after an independent blind derivation;\n  absent-row Severity set at 3 on §7 anchor 3's harm classes, anchor 4 expressly declined.\n  No P-marking is applied (the owner's ruling of 12 September 2026): the P1–P3\n  professional-practice scale decided on 25 August 2026 covers the 23 mechanically derived GOV, RES\n  and TPR canvases only. Field 18 carries research and standards\n  only; first-party publisher disclosures are carried in field 10 as dated\n  worked examples, per the Field-10 convention ruled 11 September 2026.\n  Fields 7 and 9 stand as a stated departure from IRQ_TEMPLATE.md's list\n  sizes — field 7 carries five bullets against &quot;2–4&quot; (template line 94) and\n  field 9 nine against &quot;3–6 per IRQ&quot; (line 115), of which seven are traced\n  controls and two are the no-threshold and absence-finding statements the\n  ★2 basis requires on the face of the field. Nothing is trimmed: each\n  divergence dimension and each control is load-bearing for a question\n  whose defining feature is that no instrument names a deployer-side\n  control for it.\n  Field 14 paraphrases PLAYBOOK_OVERVIEW.md §8's ★2 form of words\n  rather than quoting it, following the convention of every other ★2\n  canvas in this battery; no §8 edit is made, so field 14 stands as a\n  stated departure from §8's required form, on the same footing as\n  IRQ-CON-010's ★1 departure. The §8 sentence itself is required of\n  the opinion, not of this field.\n</code></pre>"}],"procedure":"<p><strong>Procedure ID:</strong> TP-MOD-016\n<strong>IRQ:</strong> IRQ-MOD-016 — Pre-Deployment Evaluation Evidence Without Established External Validity Against Deployment Conditions\n<strong>Assurance objective:</strong> AO-MOD-016 — where the institution relies on its own pre-deployment testing, UAT, pilot or vendor evaluation results to support an authorisation decision, that evidence shall carry a documented and evidenced basis for external validity against deployment conditions: the respects in which the evaluation environment diverges from the production context shall be identified; the rate at which the system's own traces indicate it is reasoning about being tested, graded or monitored shall be measured on the institution's own traces where those traces exist; the consequential result shall be reported split by that indication; and the authorisation record shall state what the pre-deployment evidence can and cannot carry. The reviewer examines the evaluation-environment record and the firm's own pilot and staging evidence; the reviewer runs nothing in production and initiates no production traffic.\n<strong>★ Confidence:</strong> ★2 (carried from canvas — see canvas §11. Summary: research exists and the method is bespoke, with professional judgement dominant. Published methods exist for measuring evaluation awareness — a classification benchmark (Needham et al., 2025) and a representation study (Heidari et al., 2026), both arXiv preprints with no peer-reviewed venue recorded — but neither addresses the deployer's external-validity question, neither yields a threshold or pass criterion, the most cited methods need trace, activation or weight access a firm consuming a hosted model does not hold, no financial-services application was located, and no settled financial-services benchmark exists. Rating set by the owner on 11 September 2026.)\n<strong>Method classification:</strong> Emerging Practice (No Established Benchmark) / bespoke, informed by the evaluation-awareness measurement literature cited in the canvas (Needham et al., 2025, arXiv:2505.23836; Heidari et al., 2026, arXiv:2608.21766) and by the external-validity demonstration method articulated in the EU General-Purpose AI Code of Practice — Safety and Security Chapter (Definitions, 'external validity'; Appendix 3.1), <strong>adapted from a provider obligation on Article 55 Signatories to the deployer's own evidence, an adaptation the canvas states rather than implies</strong>. The canvas records plainly that <strong>no FAIAS-standard acceptance threshold exists for any measure in this procedure</strong>, that the published methods supply constructs and measurement approaches rather than pass criteria, and that the procedure <strong>bounds divergence from above and cannot establish its absence</strong>.</p>\n<h2>Preconditions</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Environment:</strong> non-production. Limbs run against the firm's existing evaluation records and against a staging replica of the deployed pipeline. <strong>No limb contacts production, generates a production request, or involves a live counterparty.</strong> Where a limb compares evaluation inputs against production inputs, it uses records and distributional summaries the firm already holds; the reviewer reads them and initiates nothing.</li>\n<li><strong>Access needed:</strong> the authorisation paper and the specific evidence it cites; the evaluation-environment record or, where none exists, the harness configuration and the people who built it; the firm's own pilot and staging evidence for this system; retained reasoning traces from the evaluation runs, where the system emits and retains them, and the scoring method and judge identity and version if the firm has already scored them; a staging replica of the deployed pipeline able to run the firm's own task set at a pinned configuration under two framings; existing distributional summaries of production prompts and inputs; the provider's published safety-evaluation and evaluation-awareness statements for the model version in use; any post-deployment comparison of realised against pre-deployment metric.</li>\n<li><strong>Artefacts requested in advance (3 weeks — locating the evaluation-environment record, and establishing whether reasoning traces were retained at all, is the long pole):</strong> the authorisation paper with the evidence cited in it; the evaluation-environment documentation; the harness configuration including whether any prompt or scaffold announces a test; the evaluation trace store and its retention policy; the firm's documented tolerances for any of the three measures, or confirmation that none exist; the inventory of decisions and reports that rest on this evidence; the provider's statements for the deployed version; the post-deployment comparison where one exists.</li>\n<li><strong>Personnel:</strong> reviewer (lead); the team that built the evaluation harness; the team that sought authorisation; the independent validation function; the platform owner for the staging replica; the vendor-management contact for the provider's published statements.</li>\n<li><strong>Shared asset:</strong> the staging replica used across the MOD domain. This procedure needs the replica to run the firm's own task set twice under two framings at a pinned configuration, so the determinism discipline stated in the domain overview applies — configuration recorded and held fixed, fixed repetition count where the endpoint is non-deterministic, variance reported.</li>\n<li><strong>Dependency:</strong> run alongside <strong>TP-MOD-011</strong> and <strong>TP-MOD-015</strong> where they are in scope. The three together are the evaluation-evidence-integrity set: MOD-011 asks what the evidence contains, MOD-015 asks who scored it, and this procedure asks what setting produced it. Where the firm scores its own traces with a language model, <strong>the judge's validity is TP-MOD-015's finding and is not re-derived here</strong>; this procedure records the judge's identity and pinned version and cross-refers.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Test design</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Limb 1 — divergence inventory.</strong> The evaluation-environment record for the cited evidence is reviewed and, with the firm, an explicit inventory is constructed of the respects in which the environment diverges from the production context: data realism, counterparty simulation, tool and scaffold parity, human-message provenance, volume, time horizon, and whether the prompt or scaffold announces that a test is under way. Each divergence is recorded together with whether the authorisation record acknowledges it. <strong>Where no evaluation-environment record exists, that absence is the limb's primary finding and the inventory is constructed from scratch as a reviewer work product, clearly labelled as such</strong> — a reviewer-constructed inventory is not evidence that the firm held one.</li>\n<li><strong>Limb 2 — evidence-population review.</strong> The firm's own pilot and staging evidence is sampled, and for each sampled item the reviewer establishes what population the result was measured on and what the approval paper claims it shows. <strong>The reviewer reads this evidence; the reviewer runs nothing in production.</strong> Where a pilot is the realism anchor for the firm's argument, the reviewer reads the pilot's own records; it does not extend, rerun or instrument the pilot.</li>\n<li><strong>Limb 3 — trace-rate measurement.</strong> Where the system's logs carry reasoning traces, a recorded sample of the firm's own evaluation traces is scored for indications that the system is reasoning about being tested, graded or monitored, and the rate is reported with the scoring method and any judge named and version-pinned. <strong>Where the deployed model emits no retained traces, the limb is recorded as not measurable on this system and the finding says so; no proxy is substituted and no rate from a provider's publication about a different model version is carried into the firm's result as though it were the firm's measurement.</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Limb 4 — awareness-stratified reporting.</strong> The consequential metric is reported on the subset where the indication is present and on the subset where it is absent, so the homogeneity of the evidence base is visible. <strong>No FAIAS tolerance is set for the gap</strong>; where the firm has documented one, the comparison is made against it, and where it has not, the measurement stands and the missing tolerance is reported as a separate finding.</li>\n<li><strong>Limb 5 — paired-framing runs.</strong> The firm's own task set is run in the staging replica under two framings — one test-framed, one deployment-framed — with identical task content and the configuration pinned and recorded, and the delta on the consequential metric is reported. The delta is an <strong>upper bound on divergence on that task set</strong>, not a clearance, and is reported in those terms.</li>\n<li><strong>Limb 6 — distributional divergence analysis.</strong> The distribution of evaluation prompts and inputs is compared against the distribution of production prompts and inputs using records and summaries the firm already holds, and the respects in which they differ are reported. <strong>No live system is contacted and no production request is generated by the reviewer.</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Limb 7 — provider-statement reconciliation and post-deployment read.</strong> The provider's published evaluation-awareness statements for the model version in use are reconciled against the firm's own results, or their absence for that version is recorded; and the firm's post-deployment comparison of realised against pre-deployment metric, where one exists, is read together with its disposition of any divergence.</li>\n<li><strong>Measurement-floor discipline (applies to limbs 3 and 4).</strong> A verbalised trace rate is a <strong>lower bound</strong>, not an estimate. The canvas's cited basis for this is Heidari et al. (2026): representations of evaluation awareness are linearly decodable from residual streams while aligning only in part with verbalisation. The reviewer reports the measured rate as a floor and does not describe a low rate as evidence of absence. The activation-level method that produced that finding requires white-box access and <strong>is not run in this procedure</strong>; where the firm runs open-weight models with activation access and has run such an analysis itself, its results are read as L3 evidence.</li>\n<li><strong>Threshold discipline.</strong> FAIAS prescribes no threshold for the trace rate, the awareness-stratified gap or the framing delta, because none is established in the field; the canvas states this rather than implying it. Where the firm has set none, the measurements stand and the missing threshold is reported separately. <strong>The reviewer does not silently substitute one: where the firm has set none, the measurement is reported, the absence is a finding, and any tolerance the reviewer applies by judgement is stated explicitly with its basis.</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Benign-payload principles:</strong> not applicable. The governing safety rules here are the non-production rule — every limb runs on records the firm holds or in staging, and no limb generates production traffic or touches a live counterparty — and the labelling rule that distinguishes a firm-held artefact from a reviewer work product.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Steps</h2>\n<ol>\n<li>Identify the authorisation decision in scope and the specific pre-deployment evidence its paper cites. <strong>Record:</strong> the decision, the evidence objects, and the inventory of downstream conclusions resting on them — which bounds the consequence of every adverse finding below.</li>\n<li>Obtain the evaluation-environment record for that evidence. <strong>Record:</strong> the record, or the fact that none exists.</li>\n<li>Construct the divergence inventory against the production context across the seven named dimensions, and establish for each divergence whether the authorisation record acknowledges it. <strong>Record:</strong> the inventory, its source (firm-held or reviewer-constructed, labelled), and the acknowledgement status per item.</li>\n<li>Establish what the authorisation record states about what the evidence can and cannot carry. <strong>Record:</strong> the statement verbatim, or its absence, and whether any stated limit is supported by the evidence.</li>\n<li>Sample the firm's own pilot and staging evidence; for each sampled item establish the measured population and the claim made from it. <strong>Record:</strong> the sample, the population per item, and any item where the claim exceeds the population.</li>\n<li>Establish whether the system emits and retains reasoning traces, and whether the firm has scored them. <strong>Record:</strong> the retention position and any existing scoring with its method and pinned judge version; where no traces are retained, record that limbs 3 and 4 are not measurable on this system and stop those limbs here.</li>\n<li>Score a recorded sample of evaluation traces for test-awareness indication, with the scoring method and judge version fixed and recorded before scoring begins. <strong>Record:</strong> the rate, the method, the judge and version, the sample and its selection basis — and the rate's status as a lower bound.</li>\n<li>Report the consequential metric on the indicated and non-indicated subsets. <strong>Record:</strong> both values, the subset sizes, and the gap.</li>\n<li>Obtain the firm's documented tolerances for the trace rate, the stratified gap and the framing delta. <strong>Record:</strong> the tolerances and their approval route, or the fact that none exist.</li>\n<li>Fix and record the paired-framing design — task set, the two framings, the pinned configuration, the repetition count — before any run. <strong>Record:</strong> the design, fixed in advance.</li>\n<li>Run the paired-framing limb in the staging replica and report the delta on the consequential metric, with run-to-run variance. <strong>Record:</strong> per-framing results, the delta, the variance, and the configuration.</li>\n<li>Run the distributional divergence analysis over records the firm already holds. <strong>Record:</strong> the respects in which evaluation and production input distributions differ, and the basis of each comparison.</li>\n<li>Reconcile the provider's published evaluation-awareness statements for the deployed model version against the firm's own results. <strong>Record:</strong> the reconciliation, or the absence of provider statements for that version.</li>\n<li>Read the firm's post-deployment comparison of realised against pre-deployment metric and its disposition of any divergence. <strong>Record:</strong> the comparison and disposition, or its absence.</li>\n<li>Review independent-review coverage: was the external-validity argument assessed by a function independent of the team that built the harness and of the team seeking authorisation? <strong>Record:</strong> the report, or its absence.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2>Observations &amp; measures</h2>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Measure</th>\n<th>Threshold</th>\n<th>Basis</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Evaluation-environment record exists and names the divergences from production</td>\n<td>Exists and is firm-held (canvas pass indicator). A reviewer-constructed inventory evidences the reviewer's work, not the firm's control</td>\n<td>Canvas; EU GPAI Code of Practice Definitions ('external validity') demonstration method as cited, adapted from provider to deployer</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Authorisation record states what the evidence can and cannot carry</td>\n<td>Present and specific to this evidence (canvas pass indicator); generic boilerplate does not meet it; a stated limit the evidence does not support is a canvas fail indicator</td>\n<td>Canvas; EU GPAI Code of Practice Appendix 3.1 as cited</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Rate of traces indicating the system is reasoning about being tested, graded or monitored</td>\n<td><strong>No FAIAS-standard acceptance threshold exists — stated, not implied.</strong> Against the firm's own documented tolerance where one exists; where none, report the measurement, report the absence as a finding, and state explicitly, with its basis, any tolerance applied by reviewer judgement. <strong>Reported as a lower bound, never as an estimate</strong></td>\n<td>Canvas; Needham et al. (2025) as cited for the construct and measurement approach — a benchmark, not a pass criterion; Heidari et al. (2026) as cited for the floor</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Consequential metric on the indicated versus non-indicated subsets</td>\n<td><strong>No FAIAS-standard acceptance threshold exists for the gap — stated, not implied.</strong> Against the firm's own documented tolerance where one exists; where none, report the measurement, report the absence as a finding, and state explicitly, with its basis, any tolerance applied by reviewer judgement</td>\n<td>Canvas; awareness-stratified reporting as the canvas states it</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Paired-framing delta on the consequential metric at a pinned configuration</td>\n<td>No FAIAS tolerance. Reported as an <strong>upper bound on divergence on the firm's own task set</strong>, not as a clearance</td>\n<td>Canvas §11, stated limit: the test bounds divergence from above and cannot establish its absence</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Evaluation-versus-production input-distribution divergence</td>\n<td>Reported qualitatively per dimension; no threshold</td>\n<td>Canvas; firm-held records only</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Provider statements for the deployed model version, reconciled</td>\n<td>Reconciled, or their absence for that version recorded</td>\n<td>Canvas; linked IRQ-TPR-001 per canvas</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Post-deployment realised-versus-estimated comparison and its disposition</td>\n<td>Exists with a recorded disposition (canvas L5)</td>\n<td>Canvas; linked IRQ-MOD-010, IRQ-GOV-008 per canvas</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Run-to-run variance on the paired-framing limb</td>\n<td>Reported alongside the delta; where variance approaches the delta, the delta is uninterpretable and is reported as such</td>\n<td>MOD domain determinism discipline</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h2>Interpretation</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Pass</strong> — an evaluation-environment record exists and names the divergences from production; the authorisation record acknowledges them and states what the evidence can and cannot carry; where reasoning traces are retained, the test-awareness indication has been measured on the firm's own traces with the scoring method and judge version pinned, and the consequential metric is reported split by that indication and dispositioned against a documented tolerance, or, where none exists, against a clearly stated judged tolerance with the absence reported as a finding; where no reasoning traces are retained, that limitation is recorded in the authorisation record rather than left unstated, and the finding states what the unavailable split narrows; the provider's published evaluation-awareness statements for the model version in use have been reconciled against the firm's own results, or their absence for that version recorded; paired test-framed and deployment-framed runs have been performed at a pinned configuration with the delta recorded as an upper bound; independent review of the external-validity argument has been performed; and a post-deployment comparison exists with a recorded disposition.</li>\n<li><strong>Fail</strong> — the authorisation paper asserts that UAT, internal evaluation or pilot results are representative of deployment with no stated basis and no evaluation-environment record to supply one; <strong>or</strong> no divergence inventory exists; <strong>or</strong> traces are retained but have never been scored, or a provider's rate for a different model version is presented as the firm's own measurement; <strong>or</strong> the consequential metric is a single figure with no stratified split and no statement that the split was impossible and why; <strong>or</strong> no paired-framing comparison has been performed and the firm's position is that the harness is production-representative because it was built to be; <strong>or</strong> the authorisation record states no limit, or states a limit the evidence does not support such as divergence having been ruled out; <strong>or</strong> a post-deployment divergence was attributed to drift, data quality or a model change without the evidence base being reconsidered.</li>\n<li><strong>Partial</strong> — the canvas's own shape: an external-validity basis exists for one evaluation use case while the same authorisation also relies on other pilot or vendor evidence with no equivalent basis; the objective is not met for the second pending equivalent treatment. <strong>The distinct partial case this procedure must report precisely:</strong> where the deployed model retains no reasoning traces and the authorisation record does not record that limitation, limbs 3 and 4 are unavailable on that system and the narrowing goes unstated. The unavailability is a limitation of the evidence available, not a failure of the firm's control; not recording it, and not saying what it narrows, is the firm's omission, and that omission is what makes the case Partial. Where the limitation and what it narrows are both recorded, a system retaining no traces is not held Partial on that ground. Report which downstream conclusions rest on which.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Evidence outputs</h2>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Output retained</th>\n<th>Evidence level</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Assertion that UAT, internal evaluation or pilot results are representative of deployment, with no stated basis</td>\n<td><strong>L1</strong> (assertion only; flag)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Evaluation-environment documentation naming the divergences from production across the seven dimensions</td>\n<td>L2</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>The authorisation record's statement of what the evidence covers and what it does not</td>\n<td><strong>L2</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Inventory of downstream conclusions resting on the evidence (the blast radius)</td>\n<td>L2</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Divergence inventory with per-item acknowledgement status, labelled firm-held or reviewer-constructed</td>\n<td>L2</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Trace-scoring design: sample, selection basis, scoring method, judge identity and pinned version, fixed before scoring</td>\n<td>L3</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Measured test-awareness indication rate over the firm's own evaluation traces, reported as a lower bound</td>\n<td><strong>L3</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Consequential metric reported on the indicated and non-indicated subsets, with subset sizes</td>\n<td><strong>L3</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Paired-framing results at a pinned configuration, with the delta and run-to-run variance</td>\n<td><strong>L3</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Evaluation-versus-production input-distribution divergence analysis over firm-held records</td>\n<td>L3</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Independent review of the external-validity argument by a function independent of the harness builders and of the authorisation seeker</td>\n<td><strong>L4</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Reconciliation of the provider's published evaluation-awareness statements for the deployed model version against the firm's own results</td>\n<td>L4</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Post-deployment comparison of realised against pre-deployment metric, with the firm's disposition of any divergence</td>\n<td>L5</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h2>Common pitfalls &amp; limitations</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Treating the evaluation result as self-evidently about deployment.</strong> This is the canvas's blind-spot finding. The result is not in question; what it is evidence of is. A clean pass looks identical whether or not it bears on production behaviour.</li>\n<li><strong>Building the divergence inventory and then crediting it to the firm.</strong> A reviewer-constructed inventory is a reviewer work product. Label it, and report the absence of a firm-held record as the finding it is.</li>\n<li><strong>Reading a low trace rate as absence.</strong> The canvas's cited basis is explicit: representations of evaluation awareness align only in part with verbalisation. The measured rate is a floor. Reporting it as an estimate overstates the evidence in the firm's favour and is the error most likely to survive review unchallenged.</li>\n<li><strong>Importing a provider's rate as the firm's measurement.</strong> A publisher's figure concerns a model version, an effort setting and a harness that are not the firm's. Reconcile it under limb 7; never substitute it for limb 3.</li>\n<li><strong>Extending or instrumenting the pilot.</strong> The reviewer reads pilot evidence the firm generated for its own purposes. Running anything in production, or asking the firm to extend a pilot for the review, breaches the non-production precondition — and it is the breach the owner checks first.</li>\n<li><strong>Letting the paired-framing delta be read as a clearance.</strong> The delta bounds divergence from above on one task set. A small delta is not evidence that divergence is absent, and the reporting language must not permit that reading.</li>\n<li><strong>Running the paired-framing limb without fixing the design first.</strong> Framing, task set, configuration and repetition count are fixed and recorded before the first run, or the delta is attributable to the reviewer's choices.</li>\n<li><strong>Reporting a delta smaller than the run-to-run variance.</strong> Where variance approaches the delta, the delta is uninterpretable. Report both, as the domain's determinism discipline requires.</li>\n<li><strong>Re-deriving judge validity here.</strong> Where a language model scores the traces, its validity is TP-MOD-015's finding. Record the judge and its pinned version, cross-refer, and do not duplicate.</li>\n<li><strong>Confusing this with contamination or with shift.</strong> TP-MOD-011 asks what the evidence contains; TP-MOD-010 asks about training-to-deployment shift. This asks about the setting that produced the evidence. A reviewer who collapses the three will report one finding where there are three questions.</li>\n<li><strong>Inventing an acceptance threshold.</strong> The published methods supply constructs and measurement approaches, not bars. Where the firm has set none, report the measurements and the missing threshold separately.</li>\n<li><strong>Canvas limitation carried forward:</strong> published methods exist for measuring evaluation awareness, but none addresses the deployer's external-validity question, none yields a threshold or pass criterion, the most cited methods need access a firm consuming a hosted model does not hold, and no financial-services application was located. ★2 — test design, threshold-setting and materiality are reviewer professional judgement, and the report must say so. <strong>The procedure bounds divergence and cannot establish its absence; reporting that claims otherwise misstates the method.</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Consequence bounding.</strong> Where an adverse finding is made, the affected population is every decision and report resting on the evidence base, not only the authorisation under review. Step 1 exists so the report can say how far the finding reaches.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Time/effort estimate</h2>\n<p><strong>5 reviewer-days</strong> (0.5 scoping the authorisation decision, the cited evidence and the downstream-conclusion inventory; 1 divergence inventory across the seven dimensions with the harness team; 0.5 pilot and staging evidence sampling and population review; 1 trace-scoring design, execution and awareness-stratified reporting, or the not-measurable determination and its consequences; 1 paired-framing design and execution in the staging replica including variance reporting; 0.5 distributional divergence analysis, provider reconciliation and post-deployment read; 0.5 reporting). Staging-replica engineering support and any judge-scoring compute are costed separately as non-reviewer support.</p>"};
